| # The same rounding as an-archive-member-is-current-in-the-second-it-was-filed,
| # reached through GNU Make's own built-in `(%): %` rule rather than through one
| # the makefile writes. Nothing here says how a member is made, so the built-in
| # rule is what decides that `mylib.a(a.o)` is current — and being current, its
| # `$(AR)` line never runs, which is what makes this gate the timestamps rather
| # than ar.
| #
| # `a.o` was written .7 of the second the index dates the member to, and
| # `mylib.a` .9 of it. Read as the target of the rule that would file it the
| # member is the end of that second and so newer than `a.o`; read as `mylib.a`'s
| # prerequisite it is the start of it and so older than the archive. Both
| # readings have to be the right way round for this build to have nothing to do.
| mylib.a: mylib.a(a.o)
| 	@echo "refiled" >> log
file a.o kept 1
| aaa
